# Mira #30 (2026-05-21, UPDATED 2026-06-13): in-process numba blockmedian
# (gmt_blockmedian_py) is byte-identical to `gmt blockmedian -bi3d -bo3d -r`
# (13/13 parity tests on real RS2 trans.dat). The 2026-05-21 numbers below
# showed it 4-7x SLOWER than gmt at dem2topo_ra's 1-pt/bin density:
#     RS2_SLC_Hawaii  (978k rows, 964k bins → 1.0 row/bin): gmt 0.6s vs py 3.0s
#     ALOS4_Pinon     (6.4M rows, 6.2M bins → 1.0 row/bin): gmt 4.4s vs py 19.3s
#
# 2026-06-13 (Mira #67): added an n==1 fast path to the
# `_per_bin_median` numba kernel. Profiling on this same RS2
# trans.dat (978883 rows -> 966217 bins, 99.97% singletons) found the
# old kernel spent ~0.24s of its ~0.32s total in three np.empty()
# allocations per bin x 966k bins -- pure allocation overhead for a
# 1-element "sort". The fast path writes the singleton straight to
# out_{x,y,z} and `continue`s, skipping all three allocations.
# A bin with n==1 has only one point, so its median IS that point;
# GMT's blockmedian has no other side effect at n=1 (verified by the
# existing test_single_point_bin parity test, which still passes).
#
# Re-measured on this host (48 cores, NUMBA_NUM_THREADS unset):
#     RS2_SLC_Hawaii (978883 rows, 966217 bins, 1pt/bin):
#         gmt subprocess: ~0.39s    py (before fix): ~0.35s
#         py (after fix): ~0.11s    -> py now ~3.5x FASTER than gmt
#     Mira #25 canonical dense case (9M rows, 50000 bins, ~180pt/bin):
#         py (before fix): ~3.25s   py (after fix): ~3.25s (unchanged,
#         as expected -- dense bins never hit the n==1 fast path)
#
# 13/13 byte-parity tests in bin_py/tests/test_gmt_blockmedian_py.py
# still pass (cache cleared + rerun) after this change.
#
# The kernel fix closes the 1-pt/bin performance gap (py now beats gmt
